# Keys trusted to sign CieloTrack receiver releases, in ssh allowed_signers format.
#
# provision.sh copies this to /etc/cielotrack/allowed_signers at install time, and the
# updater verifies release tags against that pinned copy — never against this file.
# The distinction is the whole point: if the updater trusted the copy inside the
# repository it is about to install, anyone able to push a tag could also push the key
# that vouches for it, and the signature would prove nothing.
#
# Pinning at install time means trust is decided once, when a person chose to clone
# this repository, rather than silently re-decided every night. Rotating a key is
# therefore a deliberate act: it needs updating /etc/cielotrack/allowed_signers on each
# receiver, which is inconvenient exactly in proportion to how serious it is.
#
# This key signs releases and does nothing else. It is deliberately not the key that
# authenticates to GitHub or to any server: those are used constantly and live on
# machines that are used for other things, and a single key doing both jobs means one
# compromise lets an attacker push code, sign it as a release, and have every receiver
# install it as root the same night.
drone@station.local ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIPGbVBwXNQmfX6pwcUL/6uByY7zQ2vpmfxy1QrrIAK/B
